Block-layer test images, made by make-fixtures.sh (as root, with cryptsetup 2.7, lvm2 2.03 and
Python's cryptography package; no device-mapper). The script's header says what each image
holds; every volume is filled with the pattern

    byte i = (i * 7 + i / 4096 + seed) mod 251

so tests check decrypted and mapped bytes against data no Ferric Oxide code wrote. The
passphrase is "correct horse".

lvm.img          LV "data" over two extent runs (seed 4); LUKS2, 512-byte sectors, in LV
                 "sealed" (seed 5)
lvm-on-luks.img  LV "inner" (seed 6) in LUKS2 with 4096-byte sectors
luks1-data.bin   the first 8 KiB of LUKS1 data (seed 3) for ../../../luks/tests/fixtures/luks1.img,
                 whose payload starts at sector 4096

Bytes no test reads (unused keyslot space, encrypted data past the first 8 KiB) are zeroed,
so the images compress well. To check against the tools as they are now:

    sudo tests/fixtures/make-fixtures.sh /some/empty/dir
    BLOCKLAYER_FIXTURES=/some/empty/dir cargo test -p ferric-blocklayer --test fixtures
